These tests require a whole GNOME session. They run in a container, by hand and in GitLab CI, see .gitlab-ci.yml.

The tests run the real session, as Ubuntu ships it, with the packages patched here. In place of GDM, they start
gnome-session --session=ubuntu, which has the user's service manager start gnome-session's services and GNOME Shell,
headless with one virtual monitor of 1920x1080 (two for restore-monitors), with the Ubuntu Dock and Ubuntu's other
extensions. The tests log in and out of it, as many times as they need, and drive it through GNOME Shell:
session_tests.py calls the methods of the extension session-test@mitar.tnode.com over GNOME Shell's Eval D-Bus
method, which the extension allows by turning on GNOME Shell's unsafe mode, only ever in the test session. The
extension clicks icons in the Ubuntu Dock, as the mouse does, runs commands as the Alt+F2 dialog does, types on a
virtual keyboard, moves windows and switches workspaces, and reports the windows with their workspaces, positions,
sizes and focus, the open dialogs and the clipboard.

The session has the settings which configure-gnome-desktop.sh sets with set_key, as far as their schemas are
installed: 12 static workspaces, and the Ubuntu Dock opening a new window of an application when clicked on a
workspace without one. prepare-container.sh installs this repository's session units, scripts and Chromium
configuration where configure-gnome-desktop.sh installs them. Each test runs as a new user with a service manager of
its own, so everything else, such as Firefox's profile, starts empty.

The container is to look like a machine booted with systemd as far as the session needs it: a cgroup delegated to
each user's service manager, journald, which keeps the session's log, and a system bus. There is neither logind
nor GDM, so gnome-session and GNOME Shell track the session themselves, as they do without logind, and GNOME Shell
does not create its lock screen, which the test extension then creates from GNOME Shell's own code. fake_logind.py
owns logind's name on the system bus, hands out inhibitors and announces a shutdown when a test asks it to, through an
interface logind does not have.

The tests:

  spawn-scopes    An application started at login by gnome-session, one started from the dash and a command run with
                  Alt+F2 are each in a scope of their own, together with the processes they start the moment they run,
                  none in GNOME Shell's unit, and logging out ends them all (patches/glib2.0/,
                  patches/gnome-shell/spawn-commands-in-scope.patch).
  text-editor     Text Editor started from the dock, a file opened from a terminal and a launch from the dock on a
                  workspace without its window each open a new window on the current workspace, focused. Text Editor
                  holds no logout for its unsaved documents. After logging in again, the windows return to their
                  workspaces without being focused and without switching the workspace, the launch opens a new window
                  where the user is, and the documents never saved have the text typed into them, also the text typed
                  right before logging out.
  ptyxis          The same for Ptyxis, whose windows return also to their positions and sizes, each with the screen it
                  showed and a new shell in the directory of the old one, with Ubuntu's .bashrc. Logging out and in
                  once more without using the windows brings each of them back again with its whole screen, followed
                  by the prompt of the shell before. Logging out while a program runs in it lists it, as Terminal, in
                  GNOME Shell's dialog.
  firefox         The same for Firefox, with links opened from a terminal with xdg-open, each opening in a new window
                  on the current workspace, and each window returning with its page. Logging out has to end Firefox
                  cleanly, with its session saved, before GNOME Shell shuts down, also after xdg-desktop-portal
                  restarted, which needs the patches of patches/xdg-desktop-portal-gtk/ and quit-at-session-end.patch
                  of patches/firefox/. Otherwise the next start recovers from a crash, and the window opened for the
                  launch after logging in replaces the first restored one. No restored window may demand attention,
                  which needs wayland-session-restore-no-activation.patch of patches/firefox/.
  chromium        Chromium's helpers are in the scope its launch put it into, while the browser process moves into a
                  scope of its own. Logging out ends it without a crash and with the exit type SessionEnded, and its
                  windows return to their workspaces, positions and sizes.
  chromium-quit   Ctrl+Q quits Chromium through this repository's extension and native messaging host as a logout does,
                  and the next start restores its windows to their workspaces.
  shutdown        stop-application-scopes-before-shutdown.service holds a delay inhibitor, and when logind announces
                  a shutdown, Chromium's browser process exits before the inhibitor is released, without a crash.
  lock-screen     Without reserve-space@mitar.tnode.com, locking the screen resizes a maximized window, as GNOME Shell
                  disables the dock. With the extension enabled at login, as configure-gnome-desktop.sh enables it, the
                  window keeps its size through locking and unlocking. The locked screen offers no screenshots
                  (patches/gnome-shell/no-screenshots-while-locked.patch).
  workspace-attention
                  A Text Editor window on another workspace which demands attention does not move the user to its
                  workspace when switching to a workspace with windows or without, which needs
                  patches/gnome-shell-ubuntu-extensions/. The test sets the mark through GNOME Shell; mutter sets it
                  on a window whose activation it refuses.
  portal-inhibit  portal_inhibit.py inhibits logging out through the Inhibit portal and closes the request right after,
                  as GTK does when an application withdraws an inhibition right away, 200 times: every Close succeeds,
                  and no inhibitor of gnome-session is left behind (patches/xdg-desktop-portal/).
  overview-thumbnails
                  After logging in again, the overview's workspace thumbnails show the Ptyxis windows restored to
                  workspaces not visited yet, each with its size, which needs patches/mutter/hidden-window-updates.patch.
  restore-monitors
                  With a second monitor, Ptyxis windows maximized and tiled on either monitor return to their
                  monitors, workspaces, states and geometries after logging in again, also on workspaces not visited
                  yet, the overview's workspace thumbnails show them, and visiting their workspaces shows them
                  unchanged, which needs restore-target-monitor.patch, restore-maximized-client-monitor.patch and
                  hidden-window-updates.patch of patches/mutter/. GNOME Shell has the second virtual monitor from a
                  drop-in the test writes for the test user, and the test tiles with mutter's keybindings, with
                  tiling-assistant disabled as configure-gnome-desktop.sh disables it, since tiling-assistant leaves
                  a tiled window floating.
  restart-bus     Logging out restarts the session bus also while the start of a user service which needs it waits in
                  a queued job, as the start of xdg-desktop-portal does when Desktop Icons NG starts its process again
                  while GNOME Shell shuts down, which needs
                  patches/gnome-session/restart-dbus-replace-queued-jobs.patch. Otherwise the next login uses the old
                  bus.

The tests of the global menu, gnome-extensions/global-menu@mitar.tnode.com with patches/mutter/, each install the
extension for the test user and enable it at login, with the tap of the left Alt sending XF86Launch8:

  global-menu-login
                  Signal started at login registers its menu with global-menu-registrar, which owns
                  com.canonical.AppMenu.Registrar from before the applications started at login start, and exports it.
  global-menu-gtk3, global-menu-gtk4, global-menu-qt
                  The window of apps/menuapp.py hides its menu bar, and the top bar shows the application's name and
                  File, Edit and View. Alt+F opens File with the keyboard focus on its first item, Right and Left move
                  between the menus, Escape closes them, and F10 and a tap of the left Alt open the menu of the
                  application's name, from which Right moves to File. Return, and clicks on items, also in the
                  submenu of View, activate them, a checked item shows its mark, and the window never loses its active
                  state or its keyboard focus meanwhile.
  global-menu-app-name
                  Ptyxis, which has no menus, has the menu of its name, which a tap of the left Alt opens, with New
                  Window, the items of the window and Quit, and Minimize there minimizes the window.
  global-menu-signal, global-menu-vscode, global-menu-chromium, global-menu-firefox, global-menu-libreoffice
                  The application's menus and their keys, as for the test window, and an item which acts on the
                  window: Toggle Full Screen in Signal and Full Screen in Visual Studio Code, which act only when
                  Electron has a focused window, and New Window in the others. Firefox exports its menus without
                  mnemonics, so there Alt+F stays with Firefox, and the keys are checked from the menu of the
                  application's name.
  global-menu-overflow
                  The test window with 14 further menus, more than fit into the top bar: the menus which fit keep their
                  full width, the others are submenus of the overflow button, Alt with the mnemonic of such a menu opens
                  its submenu there, and its item acts when clicked. With an actor of 300 pixels added to the top bar
                  before the menus, more of them move into the overflow button, and back once it is gone.
  global-menu-lock
                  Locking the screen hides the global menu and unlocking shows it again, while the GTK window keeps its
                  menu bar hidden throughout. Disabling the extension shows the window's menu bar again, and
                  global-menu-registrar gives up its name.

The tests text-editor, ptyxis and firefox also check that no restored window demands attention. The test chromium
only records such windows in its result.json: Chromium asks mutter to activate every window it restores, without an
event of the user behind the request, so mutter marks each of them as demanding attention (Chromium issue 582928432),
and no patch here changes that.

After every test, the script checks that no unit of the user's service manager failed, as at-spi-dbus-bus.service
does at a logout without systemd/user/at-spi-dbus-bus.service.d/.

For the tests, prepare-container.sh also configures the browsers differently from a fresh profile: Firefox restores
the last session at startup and opens links from other applications in a new window, as the user's profile does, and
saves its session every second, through /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json. Chromium starts without the questions of a
first start, and continues where it left off, which is not its default, through /etc/chromium.d/session-test and a
policy. The tests serve their pages from a web server on localhost, and set Firefox as the default browser.

prepare-container.sh installs the session and the patched packages, so it needs the .deb files built here, and
Chromium from the xtradeb repository in sources.list.d/. The tests of the global menu need further applications, which
it installs when SESSION_TEST_APPS names them: signal for global-menu-login and global-menu-signal, gtk for the tests
of GTK and global-menu-overflow, qt, vscode and libreoffice. run-session-tests.sh then runs the tests. Both run as root
in a privileged container, and refuse to run anywhere else. With rootless Docker, from the root of the repository, and
the packages of the local repository:

  docker run --rm --privileged -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" -v /usr/local/lib/debs:/debs:ro -v /tmp/session-test:/out \
    registry.gitlab.com/tozd/docker/base:ubuntu-resolute bash -c '
      apt-get update -q && apt-get install -y -q ca-certificates &&
      cp /repo/sources.list.d/xtradeb-ubuntu-apps-resolute.sources /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ &&
      /repo/tests/session/prepare-container.sh /debs &&
      /repo/tests/session/run-session-tests.sh /out/run spawn-scopes text-editor ptyxis firefox chromium \
        chromium-quit shutdown lock-screen workspace-attention portal-inhibit overview-thumbnails \
        restore-monitors restart-bus'

A later DEBS_DIR replaces packages of the same name of an earlier one, so a build to test can follow the local
repository. prepare-container.sh can run again on a prepared container, for example without DEBS_DIR to install a
changed file of this repository. It then keeps every installed package as it is, as apt-get would otherwise replace
a package built here with Ubuntu's package of the same version. The tests need a container prepared only once, so a
container saved after prepare-container.sh can run them again and again.

The driver runs outside of the user's service manager, as a login session does. From there, systemd-run --scope
cannot move a process into a scope, because the user's service manager has no permission to move it. A test
therefore runs a command which a terminal would run in a service of the user's service manager instead, with
KillMode=process, so that the processes the command starts keep running when it exits, as in a terminal.

Each check prints a line starting with PASS or FAIL, and the script exits with 0 only when all passed. OUT_DIR
receives a directory for each test, with result.json, the journal written meanwhile, the output of gnome-session's
leader at each login, and the settings of configure-gnome-desktop.sh which could not be set, and logind.json, with
the inhibitors fake_logind.py handed out and the shutdowns it announced. A test given more than once reports each
further run in a directory whose name ends with the run's number.

The container differs from a desktop: the session renders in software without a GPU, input comes from a virtual
keyboard and not from a person, and a lock screen without GDM unlocks without asking for the password.
